(2,
 'Your body is continuing to prepare for ovulation and possible conception. During Week 2, hormones continue changing, an egg begins maturing in the ovary, and the uterine lining thickens to support a future pregnancy. Ovulation may happen toward the end of this week for many women.',
 'Your body is entering the fertile phase of the menstrual cycle. Hormone levels begin increasing to support ovulation. Some women may notice increased energy, changes in cervical mucus, mild bloating, or slight pelvic discomfort. These changes are often related to ovulation preparation.',
 '["Mild bloating","Increased vaginal discharge","Breast sensitivity","Mild pelvic discomfort","Increased energy","Changes in mood"]',
 '["Folic acid","Iron","Protein","Calcium","Omega-3 fats","Hydration"]',
 '["Leafy greens","Fruits","Whole grains","Nuts and seeds","Eggs","Yogurt and dairy","Lentils and beans"]',
 '["Smoking","Alcohol","Excess caffeine","Highly processed foods"]',
 '["Gentle exercise or walking","Prioritize sleep","Manage stress","Stay hydrated","Track ovulation if desired"]',
 '["Continue prenatal vitamins","Focus on balanced meals","Reduce stress where possible","Spend time relaxing and resting"]',
 'Trying to conceive can bring many emotions. You may feel excited, hopeful, impatient, anxious, or emotional. Some women feel pressure during ovulation tracking or timing. Gentle reminder: You do not need to control every detail perfectly.',
 '["Mild bloating","Increased discharge","Emotional changes","Slight cramping","Increased energy","Changes in appetite"]',
 '["Continue prenatal vitamins","Stay hydrated","Eat balanced meals","Track ovulation if desired","Prioritize sleep","Reduce stress where possible","Limit smoking/alcohol/caffeine","Gentle physical activity"]',
 '["Severe pelvic pain","Unusually heavy bleeding","Fever","Severe dizziness","Ongoing fertility concerns","Significant menstrual irregularities"]',
 '["Reduce emotional pressure","Support healthy routines","Help with stress management","Participate in planning together","Offer reassurance and patience"]',
 'Your body is doing important preparation work this week, even if you cannot see it yet. Small healthy habits, rest, nourishment, and emotional care all help support your pregnancy journey beautifully. You are already taking meaningful steps toward caring for yourself and your future baby.',
 '🌱', 'Sesame seed', 0.2),

(3,
 'This is often the week when conception may happen. During Week 3, ovulation usually occurs, sperm and egg may meet, fertilization can happen, and the fertilized egg begins dividing into many cells. Your future baby is now a tiny group of rapidly growing cells traveling toward the uterus. Important genetic information like eye color, hair color, and biological sex is already determined at fertilization.',
 'Your body is going through important hormonal and reproductive changes. After ovulation, progesterone levels rise, the uterine lining thickens further, and the body prepares for possible implantation. Most women still do not feel pregnant yet during this stage.',
 '["Mild bloating","Breast tenderness","Fatigue","Increased discharge","Mild cramping","Mood changes"]',
 '["Folic acid","Protein","Iron","Calcium","Omega-3 fats","Hydration"]',
 '["Leafy greens","Fruits","Lentils and beans","Eggs","Nuts and seeds","Dairy products","Whole grains"]',
 '["Smoking","Alcohol","Excess caffeine","Raw or undercooked foods"]',
 '["Prioritize sleep","Gentle exercise or walking","Stay hydrated","Reduce stress","Eat balanced meals regularly"]',
 '["Continue prenatal vitamins","Avoid excessive exhaustion","Focus on emotional wellbeing","Rest when needed"]',
 'This stage can feel exciting, emotional, uncertain, hopeful, or stressful. Some women feel deeply aware of every small body sensation during the two-week wait. Others try not to think about it constantly. Both are completely valid.',
 '["No symptoms at all","Mild bloating","Emotional changes","Breast tenderness","Fatigue","Light cramping"]',
 '["Continue prenatal vitamins","Stay hydrated","Eat balanced meals","Prioritize sleep","Avoid smoking and alcohol","Reduce excess caffeine","Gentle physical activity","Focus on stress management"]',
 '["Severe pelvic pain","Heavy bleeding","Fever","Severe dizziness","Unusual reproductive symptoms","Ongoing fertility concerns"]',
 '["Reduce stress and pressure","Offer emotional reassurance","Support healthy habits","Be patient during uncertainty","Participate in the journey together"]',
 'Even though everything is still microscopic right now, incredible changes may already be beginning inside your body. Your body is working quietly and beautifully behind the scenes. Small moments of rest, nourishment, care, and hope all matter during this early stage of the journey.',
 '🫐', 'Blueberry', 0.3),

(4,
 'This is often the week when implantation may happen and early pregnancy officially begins. The fertilized egg, now called a blastocyst, travels into the uterus and may attach to the uterine lining. Tiny early structures begin forming that will later develop into the placenta, amniotic sac, and embryo. Your baby is still extremely tiny — about the size of a poppy seed.',
 'Your body begins producing more pregnancy hormones, especially hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin). These hormonal changes help support implantation and early pregnancy development. Some women may begin noticing very early pregnancy symptoms during this week, while others notice nothing yet. Both can be normal.',
 '["Missed period","Mild cramping","Breast tenderness","Fatigue","Bloating","Emotional changes","Increased discharge","Light spotting (implantation spotting)"]',
 '["Folic acid","Iron","Protein","Calcium","Omega-3 fats","Hydration"]',
 '["Leafy greens","Fruits","Lentils and beans","Eggs","Dairy products","Nuts and seeds","Whole grains"]',
 '["Smoking","Alcohol","Excess caffeine","Raw or undercooked foods"]',
 '["Prioritize rest","Gentle walking or stretching","Stay hydrated","Reduce stress","Eat small balanced meals"]',
 '["Continue prenatal vitamins","Avoid overexertion","Listen to your body","Rest when tired"]',
 'Week 4 can feel emotionally intense for many women. You may feel excited, nervous, overwhelmed, hopeful, or anxious. Some women may take a pregnancy test this week and begin processing the reality of pregnancy. Others may still be waiting or uncertain. All feelings are valid.',
 '["Mild cramping","Bloating","Fatigue","Breast tenderness","Emotional changes","Little or no symptoms"]',
 '["Continue prenatal vitamins","Stay hydrated","Eat balanced meals","Prioritize sleep and rest","Avoid smoking and alcohol","Reduce excess caffeine","Take pregnancy test if period is missed","Schedule first doctor appointment if needed"]',
 '["Heavy bleeding","Severe abdominal pain","Severe dizziness","Fever","Fainting","Severe one-sided pain"]',
 '["Offer emotional reassurance","Reduce stress","Support healthy habits","Celebrate small moments together","Help create a calm environment"]',
 'Even though your baby is still incredibly tiny, important changes may already be happening inside your body. This early stage can feel emotional, exciting, and uncertain all at once. You do not need to have everything figured out right now. Rest, nourishment, hydration, and gentle self-care are already beautiful ways to support your growing journey.',
 '🌱', 'Poppy seed', 0.4),

(5,
 'Your baby is growing incredibly fast this week. The tiny embryo is now about the size of a sesame seed. Important early development begins, including brain development, spinal cord formation, heart formation, and early circulatory system development. The neural tube, which later becomes the brain and spinal cord, continues developing rapidly. Tiny beginnings of arms, legs, and facial features are also starting to form.',
 'Pregnancy hormones continue rising quickly during Week 5. Your body is working hard to support implantation, hormone production, placenta development, and early pregnancy growth. Many women begin noticing stronger pregnancy symptoms this week. Fatigue may increase significantly.',
 '["Missed period","Fatigue","Breast tenderness","Bloating","Nausea beginning","Food aversions","Emotional changes","Increased urination"]',
